          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01RuTGuJrCQ9uKFSmVYYwU1u",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022pages/concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining and ranking results from multiple retrieval methods, particularly hybrid retrieval combining lexical and semantic search.\\ntimestamp: 2026-01-15T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/Ranking\\n  - concept/RAG\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Core concept from production systems; single authoritative source on hybrid retrieval pattern.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\nHybrid retrieval combines **sparse lexical search** (e.g. BM25) with **dense vector search**, then merges the two ranked result sets, often via reciprocal rank fusion, to capture both exact term matches and semantic similarity.\\n\\n### Why Hybrid Works\\n\\nHybrid retrieval consistently outperforms either method alone, particularly on queries that mix:\\n- **Rare identifiers**: error codes, product SKUs, specific reference numbers\\n- **Natural language intent**: conceptual meaning and semantic context\\n\\n### Merging Strategy\\n\\nReciprocal rank fusion is a common merge strategy that combines the rankings from both retrieval methods without requiring joint vector representations. This approach avoids the need to normalize scores across fundamentally different ranking systems.\\n\\n### Production Status\\n\\nHybrid retrieval has become the **default retrieval pattern in production RAG systems**, replacing single-method approaches in most modern implementations.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Source: eval://update-over-duplicate/sample-04\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
